---
import HomeLayout from '../layouts/HomeLayout.astro';
---

<HomeLayout>
	<main>
		<section>
			<h1>About</h1>
			<p>
				What Art MD is, the current state of this site, how the Art MD and Art
				JS libraries fit together, and how to contribute.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section>
			<h2>Intro</h2>
			<p>
				Art MD expresses structured data in Markdown with an extensible language
				that enables human and machine authoring at scale and automated
				transformations.
			</p>
			<p>
				Art MD consists of a language specification and JavaScript libraries for parsing
				Art documents into a MDAST derived AST, with an open construct registry.
				The libraries provide parsing, validation, transformation, and
				serialization of Art MD content back to markdown.
			</p>
			<p>
				This site demos Art MD features and hosts its documentation. It is a
				static website application built with Astro.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section>
			<h2>Wip</h2>
			<p>
				This site is part of the <a href="https://noodlestan.github.com/">Artificials</a> project,
				which is dedicated to generating and managing artificial-driven development environments.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/art-md">Art Constructs</a> —
					implement the next set of artificial constructs — example blocks,
					inline examples, vocabulary definitions, identifiers, and context
					symbols — and register them in the constructs package.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/art-js">Art Records</a> —
					provide an API to assert the presence of records in Art content,
					extract them from a source file, and write them back.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/art-lib">Art Lib One</a> —
					provide composable units for CLI experiences, driven by use cases
					surfaced in the CLIs under development.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/art-work">Art Work CLi One</a> —
					complete the workspace CLI with the remaining commands and
					infrastructure.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
		<section>
			<h2>Architecture</h2>
			<p>
				Art MD is implemented as a Markdown dialect: a parser and a serialiser
				operate on a set of constructs to translate between Markdown and Art
				MD's structured representation.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Core libraries</strong> — Primitives (base types and
					utilities), Constructs (the contract layer binding parser and
					serializer, with an open registry), Parser (Markdown into an
					ArtDocument), Serializer (ArtDocument back into Markdown), and Codec
					(document-level parsing and serialisation).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>CLI surface</strong> — Bin (parse, serialize, validate) and
					Codec Tests (fixture-based roundtrip suite).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Spec</strong> — the Art language specification, written in Art
					itself.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				On top of that, Art JS builds a modular pipeline for authoring,
				validating, and projecting Art MD programs — libraries and CLI tools
				such as the bundler, program executor, validator, dev server, tools,
				and language server. Most Art JS packages are currently at scaffold
				status, with records and modules support still planned.
			</p>
		</section>
		<section>
			<h2>Contribute</h2>
			<p>
				Art MD lives in its own repository and this site lives in the
				Artificials umbrella repository, which also coordinates the roadmap.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/art-md">Art MD on GitHub</a> —
					the language specification and JavaScript libraries.
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan/artificials">Artificials on GitHub</a>
					— the umbrella project hosting this website and its roadmap.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				Both repositories are MIT licensed — Copyright (c) 2026
				<a href="https://noodlestan.org/">Noodlestan</a>. Published under a
				<a href="https://noodlestan.mit-license.org/">MIT license</a>.
			</p>
		</section>
	</main>
</HomeLayout>
